CM SYNTHESIZED analog of L-com/HyperLink HG2412P "Mini Panel Antenna"
CM (2.4 GHz, 11 dBi, N-Female, https://www.l-com.com - datasheet ds_HG2412P.pdf)
CM
CM Extracted target specs from datasheet:
CM   Frequency       2400-2500 MHz (2450 MHz used here, matches datasheet's
CM                   own test/pattern frequency)
CM   Gain            11.14 dBi
CM   Polarization    Vertical or Horizontal (mechanically adjustable)
CM   Beamwidth       54 deg (H-plane) / 53 deg (V-plane)
CM   F/B ratio       ~17.7-18.7 dB
CM   VSWR            <= 1.5:1, 50 ohm, N-Female, 200 W max
CM   Radome size     4.0 x 4.0 x 4.0 in (102 x 102 x 102 mm)
CM
CM THIS IS NOT A REVERSE-ENGINEERED COPY. The real HG2412P is almost
CM certainly an internal microstrip PATCH ARRAY with a printed feed
CM network - NEC2 is a free-space wire method-of-moments engine with no
CM dielectric/substrate support, so a patch array's real internal geometry
CM cannot be built in NEC2 at all, and no per-element dimensions are
CM published anyway (only the aggregate specs above).
CM
CM Instead this file is a classic 90-degree CORNER REFLECTOR DIPOLE - a
CM well-documented, pure-wire antenna archetype that is natively NEC2-
CM buildable and lands in the same gain/beamwidth neighborhood as this
CM product (~11 dBi, ~50-55 deg beamwidths). Parameters from standard
CM corner-reflector design practice (Kraus):
CM   Dipole-to-apex spacing   S  = 0.5 wavelength
CM   Reflector wing length    L  = 1.0 wavelength (each of the 2 wings,
CM                                 measured from the apex)
CM   Reflector wing height    H  = 1.1 wavelength
CM S=0.5 lambda is a standard "sweet spot" choice: good gain without
CM excessive pattern ripple, AND it happens to pull dipole feed impedance
CM down close to 50 ohm (vs. ~73 ohm for an isolated dipole), which is a
CM nice practical match to this antenna's 50 ohm N-female feed - not a
CM coincidence, a known corner-reflector design property.
CM
CM Each flat reflector wing is approximated the standard NEC way: a grid
CM of parallel wires running parallel to the dipole (both vertical here),
CM spaced ~0.083 lambda apart (12 wires per wing, comfortably under the
CM ~0.1 lambda spacing needed for a wire grid to behave like a solid
CM reflecting sheet at this frequency).
CM
CM CAVEAT: gain/beamwidth/F-B/impedance against the datasheet targets
CM above were NOT verified by re-running NEC here. Corner reflectors are
CM fairly forgiving but expect S and/or dipole length to need a few
CM percent of retuning in NEC2/4nec2/xnec2c for a clean 50 ohm match and
CM to dial in the exact 11 dBi / beamwidth targets.
CM
CM Real product is a compact 4"x4"x4" box; this wire analog's reflector
CM footprint (~1 wavelength = 4.8" per wing) is physically larger than
CM that box - expected, since a real patch array achieves the same gain
CM in a smaller volume than a wire corner reflector can.
CM
CM Axes: boresight = -X (same convention as the other files in this set).
CM Dipole and reflector apex both centered on Y=0. VERTICAL polarization
CM (dipole along Z) at height Z = 6.0 m (change to match your mount). To
CM get HORIZONTAL polarization (this product supports both, like the
CM ZDADJ433-12YG files elsewhere in this set), rotate the whole structure
CM 90 deg about the boresight (X) axis - ask if you want that variant
CM built out as a companion file.
CM Feed = dipole (tag 1), center segment. Ground = average (er=13,
CM sigma=0.005 S/m) - swap GN line for another terrain as in the other
CM files in this set.
